/E (Randomized controlled trials \(RCTs\) provide some of the strongest evidence in this area. For example, Feng Lin et al. \(2016\) conducted a randomized controlled pilot trial examining vision-based speed-of-processing training in older adults with mild cognitive impairment. The study found improvements not only in trained domains \(processing speed and attention\) but also transfer effects to untrained domains such as working memory and instrumental activities of daily living . Neural changes in resting-state networks were also observed, suggesting possible neuroplastic effects.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
439 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (Similarly, a study protocol by Xiaojuan Shi et al. \(2023\) describes a cluster randomized controlled trial investigating chess and card-based leisure activities as a non-pharmacological intervention for cognitive function in adults over 60 . The rationale is grounded in the cognitive reserve hypothesis: intellectually stimulating activities may strengthen neural networks and delay cognitive decline.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
440 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 310 0 R
/E (A systematic review by Ai Iizuka et al. \(2019\) examined 20 intervention studies using cognitive leisure activities \(e.g., reading, writing, board games, handicrafts\). Thirteen studies reported improvements in at least one cognitive domain, particularly working memory and global cognition . However, effects were often broad rather than domain-specific, and methodological quality varied.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [26]
>>
